Finally, let's consider functions we can use to map an (open) segment to the real line and vice-versa.

- The function $y = \tan x$ bijectively (i.e., both injectively and surjectively) maps the interval $\left(-\dfrac{\pi}2,\dfrac{\pi}2\right)$ onto the interval $(-\infty,\infty) = \mathbb{R}$.![](images/0c/0c30abace6b86aa63ce3da8bafa2bb4748271fdfa3d320ee0f2c7f18fbeec998.png)
- Similarly, the function $y = \cot x$ bijectively maps the interval $\left(0, \pi\right)$ onto the interval $(-\infty,\infty) = \mathbb{R}$.![](images/8f/8f464060d171517b98dcf7cfd345420e534c349c2a98e83d2300f63f42a4132c.png)
- Finally, the function $y = \arctan x$ bijectively maps the interval $\left(-\infty, \infty \right)$ onto the interval $\left(-\dfrac\pi2,\dfrac\pi2\right)$.![](images/db/db7c994eeb92c6b2debd80129d65d20cf14dc039f9a3a3a46235f8c4054c720d.png)

In particular, this means that

$$
\begin{aligned}
\bigg\begin{vmatrix}\left(-\dfrac{\pi}{2}, \dfrac{\pi}{2} \right) \bigg & =\big & (0,\pi)\big &= \big & (-\infty, \infty) \big & = \big & \mathbb R\big\end{vmatrix}
\end{aligned}
$$
